hows a tied race with Evans up two"], "rationale": "An even district with a first-term Republican incumbent would normally be a tossup, but the favorable Democratic environment, heavy pro-Rutinel outside spending, and market pricing near 82% give the challenger a clear if modest edge. The lone public poll showing Evans ahead two keeps this at Lean rather than Likely.", "watch": "Fresh independent polls and trends in Latino turnout, which reporting says has shifted away from Trump in this district, could move the race toward a true tossup or a firmer Democratic edge.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Gabe Evans (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Colorado's 8th District is an even partisan split, and first-term Republican Gabe Evans is defending it in a year when the national climate favors Democrats. Nine professional ratings split between Tossup and Democratic-favor calls, averaging out to a narrow Democratic edge. Prediction markets put Rutinel's chances near 82%, well above what the thin polling alone would suggest.\n\nOnly one public poll exists, a Republican-sponsored survey from mid-September showing Evans ahead 48-46, a result local coverage describes as essentially tied. Money is a mixed picture: Evans holds a large cash-on-hand lead, but outside groups have spent heavily for Rutinel and against Evans. Reporting that Latino support for Trump has fallen in the district adds pressure on Evans. The forecast gives Rutinel a modest edge, with wide uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7882, "margin": 4.94}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7652, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7649, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7806, "margin": 4.5}]}